data.reasoningText:
Let me plan the component decomposition and then implement it.

## Component Plan: Product Catalog

### Component Tree
```
Catalog                    (page — owns filter/sort/page state, orchestrates layout)
├── CatalogFilters         (category checkboxes, price inputs, in-stock toggle, apply/clear buttons)
├── CatalogToolbar         (sort dropdown, showing X-Y of Z label)
├── ProductGrid            (grid of product cards)
│   └── ProductCard        (single product card: image, name, price, rating, stock badge, add to cart)
└── CatalogPagination      (previous, numbered pages, next)
```

Now I'm mapping out the data flow between components. The Catalog page will manage the paged results, filters, sorting, and pagination state, then pass relevant data down to each child component. The filter component emits callbacks when the user applies or clears filters, the toolbar notifies when sort changes, and the product cards trigger callbacks for selection or adding to cart. The pagination component receives the current page information to render the navigation controls.

I'll build these components bottom-up starting with the leaf nodes—ProductCard, CatalogFilters, CatalogToolbar, and CatalogPagination—then compose them into ProductGrid and finally the main Catalog page. I'm organizing them in a Components/Catalog folder with the page itself in Components/Pages.

